Subject: [SE4-P6] mfmediaengine: never call the application with the engine lock held

IMFMediaEngineNotify::EventNotify was called with engine->cs held from about
thirty places (the session event handler, the load handler, Play, Pause,
SetCurrentTime, SetSource, ...), and every per-frame call of the engine
takes the same lock. An application callback that runs for a long time,
takes a lock the calling thread holds, or hands the work to the calling
thread and waits, therefore stops OnVideoStreamTick, GetCurrentTime and
every other call on that engine for as long as it runs: as long as the
handler, or until the application's own timeout when it waits for the
blocked thread. A failed load, a stalled source and a buffering change all
reach the application this way. The same holds for the calls into
IMFMediaEngineExtension, which also ran under the lock.

No application code runs with the engine lock held any more.

Every notification goes through media_engine_notify(), which only appends
it, with its parameters and the raising thread, to a per-engine outbox.
Every acquisition of the lock goes through media_engine_lock() and
media_engine_unlock(). The thread that releases the lock at its outermost
level delivers the notifications it raised, in order, after leaving the
lock and before the call that raised them returns, so each notification is
still delivered by the thread that raised it (SEEKING by the time
SetCurrentTime returns, as Wine's tests expect) and the order within a
thread does not change.

The application still never sees two notifications of one engine at once:
delivery holds a per-engine recursive delivery lock, which is never taken
with the engine lock held. Work-queue callbacks (session events, sink
events, load handler) wait for it while holding no engine lock, as they
waited for the engine lock before. An application-facing call never waits
for a callback that has run for 10 ms or more: it hands its notifications
off and returns at once, and the holder delivers them, in order, before it
releases it (and no notification is left behind when the holder releases at
the same moment). The one exception is a callback that started less than
10 ms ago, which is waited for until those 10 ms are up: a handler often
signals the thread that then calls the engine while the handler is still
returning, and that call's notifications stay on its thread. A call made from inside a callback
takes the delivery lock recursively and delivers the notifications it
raises before it returns, as it did when the callback ran under the lock.

Where the code after a notification depends on what the application did in
its handler, the site delivers first and then revalidates what it read:
the buffering re-check after BUFFERINGSTARTED, the ready state between
LOADEDMETADATA and LOADEDDATA, the
SEEKED handling of a started session, the replacement check after LOADSTART
and after PURGEQUEUEDEVENTS. The extension's BeginCreateObject and
CanPlayType are called with the lock dropped.

The deliverer holds a reference on the engine and on the callback for the
whole delivery. Shutdown drops what has not been delivered and nothing is
queued after it; a delivery already in progress may finish, and Shutdown
never waits for a callback.

Diagnostics, always compiled and on the existing channel, each kind at most
one line per engine and second: a WARN at 100 ms and an ERR at 1 s when a
notification takes that long in the application; a WARN at 50 ms and an ERR
at 1 s when a call waited for the engine lock, naming the call that waited
and the site and thread that held it; a WARN at 100 ms and an ERR at 1 s
when the engine lock was held that long, naming the site that held it; and
an ERR, once per engine, if a callback is about to start with the lock held
by the calling thread (a regression guard that must never fire).

Limits. Under contention (another thread is inside a callback of the
same engine) an application-facing call returns before its notifications
are delivered, and the thread in the slow callback delivers them; without
contention, nothing changes. The load handler still holds the engine lock
while it creates the topology, so calls on the same engine wait for a load;
that wait is reported by the lock-wait diagnostic, not removed here. Calls
the engine makes into other application-supplied objects during a load (an
extension-created source, effects) are not changed.

